Subject: winemac: Fix use-after-free race in run_cocoa_app that skips [NSApp run].
Author: Alexander Theissen, athei/wine commit df416a601ed883dc654f4db0fd31976c5b245fed (2026-04-09, branch cx-26-patched), applied verbatim.
Licence: LGPL-2.1-or-later, same as Wine.
Provenance: https://github.com/athei/wine/commit/df416a601ed883dc654f4db0fd31976c5b245fed. Applied after 0001-0019 to Wine 11.19, source tarball
73eb0b02dfc0cb0ff4f4d8d36c5786261e9725ac91968ae4d4e4b9a498c4087f. Not submitted upstream by AnvilMac.
Why (AnvilMac, 2026-11-09): run_cocoa_app reads a variable on macdrv_start_cocoa_app's stack after that function may
have returned; the Cocoa event loop can then be skipped and the window never gets input. More likely when a tool
changes thread timing at startup, as x87sidecar (patch 0018) does for 32-bit games. No case seen in AnvilMac yet.

From df416a601ed883dc654f4db0fd31976c5b245fed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander Theissen <alex@theissen.io>
Date: Thu, 9 Apr 2026 19:39:48 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] winemac: Fix use-after-free race in run_cocoa_app that skips
 [NSApp run].

run_cocoa_app() accesses startup_info->success after unlocking the
condition lock that signals macdrv_start_cocoa_app() to return. Since
startup_info is a local variable on macdrv_start_cocoa_app()'s stack,
the calling thread may return and destroy the stack frame before
run_cocoa_app() reaches the `if (created_app && startup_info->success)`
check. Reading from the freed stack frame yields garbage, causing the
Cocoa event loop ([NSApp run]) to be skipped.

Without a running event loop, NSApplication never dispatches keyboard
or mouse events. The window renders normally but is permanently
unresponsive to input.

The race is timing-dependent and unlikely under normal conditions, but
becomes reliably reproducible when external factors (such as ptrace
from a debugger or JIT tool) alter thread scheduling during startup.

Fix by saving startup_info->success to a local variable before the
unlock, similar to how the lock itself is already retained to prevent
a mid-unlock deallocation.
---
 dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_main.m | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_main.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_main.m
index 869b3c73e2e..44c7e0fcb45 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_main.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_main.m
@@ -65,6 +65,7 @@ static void run_cocoa_app(void* info)
     struct cocoa_app_startup_info* startup_info = info;
     NSConditionLock* lock = startup_info->lock;
     BOOL created_app = FALSE;
+    BOOL success = FALSE;
 
     @autoreleasepool
     {
@@ -87,6 +88,10 @@ static void run_cocoa_app(void* info)
             startup_info->success = TRUE;
         }
 
+        /* Save success to a local before unlocking: startup_info points to
+           the caller's stack frame, which may be gone once we unlock. */
+        success = startup_info->success;
+
         /* Retain the lock while we're using it, so macdrv_start_cocoa_app()
            doesn't deallocate it in the middle of us unlocking it. */
         [lock retain];
@@ -95,7 +100,7 @@ static void run_cocoa_app(void* info)
         [lock release];
     }
 
-    if (created_app && startup_info->success)
+    if (created_app && success)
     {
         @autoreleasepool
         {
